== 4.7 Chapter 4 Practice Drill — 40 Questions

40 questions graded in the order the CBT engine is likely to meet them. Allow 44 minutes; the key and a full solution for every question follow.

#mcq(1, [A summary should contain],
  [the writer's exact sentences],
  [the examples that make the passage vivid],
  [the claims, reasons and conclusion],
  [the reader's opinion of the passage]
)

#mcq(2, [The first thing to drop from a summary is],
  [the conclusion],
  [the writer's reason],
  [the main claim],
  [an example]
)

#mcq(3, [“In not more than twenty words” means],
  [at least twenty words],
  [about twenty words],
  [twenty lines],
  [twenty words or fewer]
)

#mcq(4, [“Summarise the reasons the writer gives for the closure.” A summary of the effects of the closure is],
  [an example],
  [exactly what is asked],
  [too short],
  [answering a different question]
)

#mcq(5, [An opening such as “The writer is of the opinion that” is a fault because it],
  [repeats the question],
  [is ungrammatical],
  [spends words on formula rather than on the point],
  [is rude]
)

#mcq(6, [The test that separates a point from an example is],
  [whether it is the first sentence],
  [whether it contains a number],
  [what happens to the argument when you delete it],
  [length]
)

#mcq(7, [A summary of a passage that lists three facts joined by *and* is weak because it],
  [uses too many verbs],
  [gives facts without the relation between them],
  [quotes the passage],
  [is too long]
)

#mcq(8, [*For example*, *such as* and *for instance* announce],
  [a claim],
  [a definition],
  [the conclusion],
  [support for a claim]
)

#mcq(9, [A quotation in a passage is usually],
  [a definition],
  [the main idea],
  [evidence to drop],
  [a point to keep]
)

#mcq(10, [“In one sentence, state the writer's view.” A sentence with two finite verbs joined by *and* is],
  [acceptable only if the question allows two points],
  [always wrong],
  [the best answer],
  [a fragment]
)

#mcq(11, [To write a point in your own words you should],
  [change the vocabulary and keep the sense],
  [shorten it by deleting verbs],
  [change every word],
  [keep the sentence and add a word]
)

#mcq(12, [A summary that includes an opinion of the reader's own is],
  [better than the passage],
  [a good conclusion],
  [an inference],
  [no longer a summary]
)

#mcq(13, [The number of points a summary needs is decided by],
  [the number of paragraphs],
  [the instruction and the passage's structure],
  [the length of the passage],
  [the examiner's mood]
)

#mcq(14, [A list of three items in a passage is usually],
  [an illustration],
  [a claim],
  [a definition],
  [the conclusion]
)

#mcq(15, [“Summarise the writer's suggestions.” An option that describes the problem is],
  [about the wrong noun],
  [too short],
  [the answer],
  [a quotation]
)

#mcq(16, [Counting the words of a summary matters because],
  [the examiner counts them twice],
  [the limit is part of the mark scheme],
  [it improves the grammar],
  [short answers look tidy]
)

#mcq(17, [In the passage “The books were not the problem; the clinic had no agreed method”, the claim is],
  [the problem],
  [the clinic],
  [the books],
  [the absence of an agreed method]
)

#mcq(18, [A summary that keeps a vivid example and drops a reason has],
  [improved on the passage],
  [written in its own words],
  [followed the instruction],
  [kept the wrong half]
)

#mcq(19, [Two points are best joined with],
  [a full stop],
  [a connector that carries meaning, such as *because*],
  [a semicolon and no word],
  [*and* repeated]
)

#mcq(20, [Which is a point rather than an example?],
  [In 2019 a sample grew a colony.],
  [A trader buys fifty tubers in April.],
  [Loading takes twenty minutes.],
  [Traders cannot plan on a week's notice.]
)

#mcq(21, [A summary answer that repeats the passage's sentences word for word],
  [loses expression marks],
  [is the safest answer],
  [is not a summary at all],
  [earns full marks]
)

#mcq(22, [The instruction “state the two reasons” tells you that the answer needs],
  [two points],
  [an example],
  [as many points as possible],
  [one point]
)

#mcq(23, [A conclusion in a passage is usually],
  [a claim the passage has argued for],
  [a definition],
  [a quotation],
  [an example]
)

#mcq(24, [Which sentence could be deleted without breaking the argument?],
  [Traders cannot plan on a week's notice.],
  [The levy rose three times in two years.],
  [A trader who buys a hundred tubers in April must know the June levy.],
  [The levy is collected weekly.]
)

#mcq(25, [A summary of an argument is complete when],
  [every sentence is represented],
  [every point is present and no evidence is],
  [it is as short as possible],
  [it uses the writer's words]
)

#mcq(26, [The noun in the instruction is important because it],
  [tells you the length],
  [fixes which part of the passage is being summarised],
  [makes the sentence grammatical],
  [tells you the tone]
)

#mcq(27, [“The council cleared the stones, so the traders lost their park.” If the passage does not say the traders lost the park, this option is],
  [a summary],
  [a lift],
  [a reversal],
  [an invention]
)

#mcq(28, [A summary written in the first person — *I think the writer means* — is],
  [acceptable],
  [wrong, because a summary reports the writer's view],
  [required],
  [the best style]
)

#mcq(29, [The commonest fault in a summary answer is],
  [counting the words],
  [using connectors],
  [too few verbs],
  [keeping evidence and dropping a point]
)

#mcq(30, [A figure such as *two thousand naira* in a passage is],
  [usually a detail],
  [a definition],
  [always the main point],
  [a connector]
)

#mcq(31, [Which is the best opening for a twenty-word summary?],
  [It is important to note that],
  [In this passage the writer],
  [The council has no plan],
  [As we have seen]
)

#mcq(32, [A summary that answers the question but exceeds the limit will],
  [lose marks for length],
  [be marked as a different question],
  [be ignored],
  [earn full marks]
)

#mcq(33, [The relation between two points is carried by],
  [their order alone],
  [a connector such as *because* or *so that*],
  [a full stop],
  [a quotation]
)

#mcq(34, [“Traders cannot plan a season on a week's notice.” This sentence is],
  [a definition],
  [a reason],
  [an example],
  [a quotation]
)

#mcq(35, [A candidate who writes a summary of the whole passage when asked for the causes has],
  [used the wrong tense],
  [answered fully],
  [used too few words],
  [answered a different question]
)

#mcq(36, [The expression *in short* in a passage usually introduces],
  [a compression of what has been argued],
  [an example],
  [a definition],
  [a contradiction]
)

#mcq(37, [A summary must be shorter than the original because],
  [the examiner counts sentences],
  [short answers are easier to mark],
  [the passage is long],
  [compression is the point of a summary]
)

#mcq(38, [When two options both summarise the passage, the better one is the one that],
  [covers every point the passage needs],
  [contains a quotation],
  [is longer],
  [repeats the passage's phrases]
)

#mcq(39, [A point stated in the passive voice — *no alternative site was provided* — is],
  [a definition],
  [not a point],
  [a point, and the hidden agent is part of it],
  [an example]
)

#mcq(40, [A summary of a passage about a clinic keeps “four exercise books” and drops “no agreed method of recording patients”. The summary has],
  [dropped both],
  [kept the evidence and dropped the claim],
  [kept the claim and dropped the evidence],
  [kept both]
)

